<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/inspection-visit.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/inspection-visit-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/inspection-visit.webp 1021w" sizes="100vw" width="1021" height="681" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">How appraisal works</span></nav><p class="kicker">Process guide</p><h1>How Insurance Appraisal Works in New Hampshire</h1><p class="lede">8 steps from the first disagreement to a signed award — what happens, who does what, and where files most often go wrong.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>Steps</small><b>8</b></li><li><small>Panel</small><b>2 appraisers + umpire</b></li><li><small>Binding</small><b>2 of 3 signatures</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Appraisal is a short, contract-based way to settle one question: how much a covered loss is worth. It isn’t a lawsuit, and it isn’t a complaint to the state. Below is how it usually runs on a New Hampshire claim, from the first letter to the check. Your own policy wording controls, so read it alongside this page.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Pin down the gap in writing</h2>
<p>Appraisal settles a disagreement over price and scope, so the first job is to make that disagreement concrete. Get the insurer’s complete estimate — every page, not the one-page summary — and set your contractor’s figures beside it. Then write a short letter to the adjuster listing what you believe is missing or underpriced, with the support attached: photos, measurements, invoices, a roofer’s note about rotted decking.</p>
<p>Plenty of New Hampshire claims stop right here, because a reasoned supplement request gets approved. If the adjuster won’t move, you now hold a clean record of exactly where the two sides part ways, which is what an appraiser needs first.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Send the appraisal demand</h2>
<p>Most appraisal clauses let either side start the process with a written demand. Keep it plain: your name, the policy and claim numbers, the date of loss, a sentence saying you and the insurer disagree on the amount of loss, and a statement that you are invoking the appraisal provision. Send it by a method that leaves a trail — certified mail plus email is a common pairing — and keep a copy.</p>
<p>Before you mail it, reread the policy for conditions that come first. Some forms expect a sworn proof of loss, records or an examination under oath before appraisal can proceed.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Appoint your appraiser</h2>
<p>Each side picks its own appraiser, and many forms ask for the name within 20 days of the demand. The policy usually calls for someone “competent and disinterested” or “independent,” which in practice means a person with real estimating knowledge and no stake in the size of the award. Ask about the fee arrangement and get it in writing before you sign. Once retained, your appraiser collects the file, reads the policy language and contacts the insurer’s appraiser to set a schedule.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Settle on an umpire</h2>
<p>The two appraisers then try to agree on a neutral umpire, commonly within 15 days under the policy wording. It pays to name the umpire early, before the hard bargaining starts, so the choice never becomes a bargaining chip. If the appraisers deadlock, either party can usually ask a court of record where the property is located to appoint one. In New Hampshire that generally means the Superior Court for the county where the home or building sits.</p>
<h2>Step 5: Inspect and price the damage</h2>
<p>Both appraisers walk the property, ideally on the same day. They measure, photograph, check attic framing after a snow-load loss, look behind trim where meltwater traveled and note anything the first adjuster missed. Each then prices the scope using current local labor and material costs.</p>
<p>Weather matters more here than in most states. A February inspection of a Lakes Region camp under three feet of snow may need a return visit after the thaw before roof slopes and siding can be measured honestly.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Reconcile the estimates item by item</h2>
<p>With two estimates in hand, the appraisers go line by line — in person or on a shared screen. Quantities get reconciled first, then unit prices, then items one side included and the other left out. A large share of appraisals finish at this stage with both appraisers signing an agreed award, and nothing reaches the umpire that the appraisers can resolve themselves.</p>
<h2>Step 7: Put the open items before the umpire</h2>
<p>Whatever the appraisers can’t agree on goes to the umpire. Each appraiser submits a written position on the open items with photos, measurements, pricing sources and code references. The umpire may inspect, ask questions or hold an informal meeting. It isn’t a trial: there is usually no sworn testimony, and courtroom rules of evidence don’t apply. The umpire then sides with one appraiser, or lands between them, on each item.</p>
<h2>Step 8: Sign the award and collect payment</h2>
<p>A written award signed by any two of the three panel members sets the amount of loss. A useful award breaks out replacement cost, depreciation and actual cash value, separates the coverages (dwelling, other structures, contents, loss of use) and notes any items left out on purpose. The insurer then pays under the policy terms, less the deductible and earlier payments, with recoverable depreciation usually released once repairs are documented.</p>
<h2>Who handles which part</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Participant</th><th>Role in the appraisal</th><th>Who pays them</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>You, the policyholder</td><td>Sends or answers the demand, gives access, supplies documents, keeps up with policy duties</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Your appraiser</td><td>Inspects, prices the scope, negotiates with the other appraiser, signs or disputes the award</td><td>You</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insurer’s appraiser</td><td>The same job on the insurer’s side</td><td>The insurer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Umpire</td><td>Decides only the items the appraisers can’t settle; signs the award with one appraiser</td><td>Split equally under most forms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Superior Court</td><td>Appoints an umpire when the appraisers deadlock and a party asks</td><td>Filing costs as the court sets them</td></tr>
<tr><td>New Hampshire Insurance Department</td><td>Takes consumer complaints and inquiries; doesn’t run or referee appraisals</td><td>No charge to consumers</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h2>Questions the panel doesn’t answer</h2>
<p>An appraisal panel values the loss. It generally doesn’t decide:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Coverage.</strong> Whether flood, surface water, wear and tear or freezing in an unheated building is excluded is a policy question, not a pricing one.</li>
<li><strong>How the insurer behaved.</strong> Delay or misrepresentation concerns belong with the Insurance Department or an attorney.</li>
<li><strong>Legal remedies.</strong> Interest, fees or damages beyond the policy are for a lawyer and, if it comes to it, a court.</li>
<li><strong>Causation, sometimes.</strong> Whether the panel can weigh what caused damage depends on the policy wording and the law; ask a New Hampshire attorney if it matters to your claim.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Fees, the clock and New Hampshire specifics</h2>
<p>Each side pays its own appraiser, and most forms split the umpire’s fee and expenses equally. Appraiser fees may be hourly, flat or tied to the award; whichever it is, it should be in writing. Our <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/gap-and-fee-estimator/">gap and fee estimator</a> helps you test whether the dispute is large enough to justify the cost.</p>
<p>The New Hampshire Insurance Department in Concord accepts complaints and questions about insurers, and RSA chapter 417 lists unfair claim settlement practices in general terms. Neither replaces appraisal, and appraisal doesn’t replace them. New Hampshire doesn’t have a broad first-party bad-faith penalty statute; remedies depend on the policy, contract law and the facts, so ask an attorney.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Watch the suit-limitation clause</b>Many property policies set a contractual window to sue — often one or two years, or a period measured from the loss. Appraisal may not pause it. Check your policy, track the date on our <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/nh-claim-deadline-calendar/">claim deadline calendar</a> and talk to a New Hampshire attorney well before it arrives.</div>
<p>Questions about a specific claim? <a href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Contact New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser</a> or read the <a href="/nh-appraisal-faq/">appraisal FAQ</a> first.</p>
</article>
